From eca048bdeb8242284f75f2c30ff0a4e2fc2f3ba5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jeroen Bobbeldijk <jeroen.bobbeldijk@doxis.com>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 08:38:32 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/5] simd: add a WebAssembly SIMD128 kernel set

Adds simd/wasm/, a set of 13 hand written SIMD128 kernels, and wires them
into the unified dispatcher as SIMD_ARCHITECTURE == WASM:

  ycc_rgb, rgb_ycc, idct_islow, fdct_islow, convsamp, quantize,
  h2v1_fancy_upsample, h2v2_fancy_upsample, h2v1_downsample,
  h2v2_downsample, huff_encode_one_block, encode_mcu_AC_first_prepare,
  encode_mcu_AC_refine_prepare

They are transliterations of the Neon kernels with the scalar arithmetic
preserved exactly, so output is bit identical to the C paths.  There is no
CPU detection to do: SIMD128 is a compile time guarantee, so jsimdcpu.c
simply reports the feature as present.

Enabled with -DWITH_WASM_SIMD=1.  Combined with -DWITH_SIMDE=1 the
translated Arm/Neon kernels are compiled in as well, but only for the
operations the wasm set does not implement, so coverage is never worse
than WITH_SIMDE alone.  That combination deliberately keeps WASM as the
base architecture rather than layering onto ARM: src/jchuff.c picks the
SIMD bit buffer width from SIMD_ARCHITECTURE, and an Arm base would hand
the wasm Huffman kernel a 32-bit buffer for its 64-bit accumulator.

Each hook is guarded by a WASM_SIMD_<KERNEL> switch defaulting to
WASM_SIMD_DEFAULT, so a build can isolate one kernel without source edits.
