From 13b23b1c2c7178d612b2169fdf9d70f6b2b9c61b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jeroen Bobbeldijk <jeroen.bobbeldijk@doxis.com>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 08:38:48 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] simd/arm: do not tie the Huffman SIMD bit buffer to the
 pointer width

On wasm32 BITS is 32, so the SIMDe build maps to aarch32, and
simd/arm/jchuff.h then gives the Huffman kernel a 32-bit bit buffer
because it declares the accumulator as size_t.  wasm has native 64-bit
integer operations regardless of pointer width, so this halves the buffer
(and doubles the number of flushes) for no reason.

src/jchuff.c already allows a SIMD bit buffer wider than size_t: that is
exactly what i386 does.  This extends the same treatment to wasm and lets
it use the aarch64 kernel, whose inline asm is already guarded with
__EMSCRIPTEN__ upstream.

Enabled with -DWASM_NEON_HUFF64=1 alongside -DWITH_SIMDE=1.  Measured on a
2,741 image corpus, the share of encode time the Neon Huffman kernel saves
against the C encoder in the same build improves from -26.6% to -6.2%
(node/arm64), -29.5% to -9.5% (node/x86_64), -15.6% to +1.1%
(wazero/arm64) and -12.2% to +7.1% (wazero/x86_64).  Negative means the
SIMD kernel is slower than the C code it replaces.

The encoded bitstream is unchanged (verified per image with CRC32C across
configurations), and the full test suite passes.

Note that even with this fix the kernel does not beat the C encoder under
V8, so the wider question of whether to dispatch it on wasm at all is
separate from this change.
